  "title": "Bitch, You're Being Worked On",
  "reviewed_on": "2026-07-30",
  "summary": "Episode 79 brings Erik Griffin into the crowded Episode 78 room and turns the phone dispute into a broader argument about attention, fame, attraction, friendship, honesty and self-control. Its best material is genuinely corrective: participants are one another's immediate audience; professional success does not excuse rude or boundary-crossing conduct; honesty does not make harmful conduct good; attraction does not require action; and a good friend can decline a sexual opportunity. Its title phrase, however, comes from reframing a woman's direct rejection as an ongoing campaign—'you're being worked on.' The archive rejects that premise: every person controls romantic and sexual participation, and someone may decline friendship without converting no into later consent. Heather's new relationship supersedes the earlier dating-show premise, and repeated sexual targeting is not indexed as playful metadata. Exact redactions protect a private former partner, a private intimate encounter, and a possible minor's social handle. Public chronology includes Griffin's Montez Walker role as Workaholics season four approached, All Def Comedy Live at Inside Jokes, Slink Johnson's Black Jesus announcement, Heather's bass work, Money Mark's site, Tinder's early adoption, Bill Poon's blog and a red-suit connection to Choe's fashion timeline. Named dialogue remains withheld.",
